      documented in `website/README.md`'s Deployment section and
      `website/.env.example`. It is deliberately *not* the built-in default,
      so a fork never inherits it as its own canonical home.
- [x] **[P3]** Vercel org slug in this file's dashboard URLs
      (`https://vercel.com/trustledger/sensebridge/...`). **Resolved
      2026-07-28** — kept as-is. It is an auth-gated operational link for the
      owner, not access, and genericizing it would break the owner's own
      navigation for no security gain. The website reference, which is what
      contributors actually consume, is the part that got documented.
- [x] **[P2]** Set `SITE_URL` in the hosting projects' own environment
      variables so production stops depending on a local `.env`.
      **Done 2026-07-28** — the owner ran the commands (the sandbox classifier
      denies the writes to an agent; it permits the reads). Verified read-only
      via the CLIs: Railway `production` → `https://sensebridge.vercel.app`,
      Railway `preview` → `https://sensebridge-preview.up.railway.app`
      (matching its own `RAILWAY_PUBLIC_DOMAIN`, so a preview describes itself
      rather than claiming to be the official site); Vercel Production has a
      `SITE_URL` entry, whose value is encrypted and could not be read back
      (`vercel env pull` is denied to an agent too). Read at **build** time —
      it takes effect on the next deploy of the branch carrying the
      `astro.config.mjs` change, not on a restart. Until that merges, the live
      site's correct URLs still come from the old hardcoded `site:` value, so
      a live `curl` is **not** evidence that this wiring works; the evidence
      is `npm run check:site-url` plus the two-directional Docker build.

