## Archived 2026-07-31

### Token/usage audit + BMAD adoption (2026-07-31, 16:44 PST)

A transcript-based audit (not inference) found no runaway process: mean context
was **161k tokens/request** over 20,223 requests, with a **~63k session-start
floor**. `task-observer` alone cost ~18.4k/session because global CLAUDE.md
§17 invoked it every session; claude-mem had spent ~3.4M tokens while broken
since 2026-07-27. Both fixed, 7 unused MCP servers removed, 26 skills parked.
BMAD was then adopted. Full write-up in `sessions/2026-07-31/1600-PST.md`;
method persisted at `~/.claude/scripts/measure-context-baseline.py`.

- [x] **[P2]** **CORRECTED 2026-07-31, 17:15 PST — the target landed; the
      17:00 "did not drop" verdict below was measuring the wrong thing.**
      `/context` reports the configuration floor directly, and it sums to
      **41.4k**: system prompt 6.3k + system tools 8.9k + custom agents 2k +
      memory files 14.3k + skills 9.9k. Against the ~63k pre-cleanup floor that
      is the projected ~21k saving, essentially on target.

      **Why the transcript measurement disagreed.** Both numbers are real and
      measure different things. `/context` reports *configuration*; the
      first-request billed context (66.9k) additionally carries per-turn
      injections — the deferred tool-name list, `SessionStart` hook output, and
      `system-reminder` blocks. Use `/context` for "did pruning work"; use the
      per-session transcript number for "what does a session actually cost".
      Do not treat the two as interchangeable, which is the error made below.

      **The single largest controllable item is `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` at 9.4k
      tokens** — 23% of the whole floor. With this repo's `CLAUDE.md` at 3.7k,
      the two instruction files are 13.1k, a third of the floor. Skills are
      9.9k across 186 entries; the heavy ones are `dataviz` ~380,
      `claude-api` ~360, `task-observer` ~330, `impeccable` ~300,
      `hyperframes` ~270, `ai-seo`/`copywriting` ~260. The ~45 `bmad-*` entries
      total only ~1.5k and every `sc:*` is under 20 — not worth touching.

      **Superseded verdict, kept for the reasoning trail.** At 17:00 this read:
      "the baseline did NOT drop."
      The three post-restart sessions opened at **64.2k / 66.9k / 66.9k**, versus
      the ~63k pre-cleanup floor. That is flat-to-slightly-worse, not the
      projected ~42k. Measured per session rather than from the script's
      "SESSION-START BASELINE" block, which averages the *last 25* sessions and
      is still dominated by pre-cleanup ones (it reported mean 68k / min 45k /
      max 83k — not a usable pass/fail signal here). Per-session first-request
      context is the right number; the one-off script that extracts it is at
      `$CLAUDE_JOB_DIR/tmp/perssn.py` in the 17:00 session log.

      **The pruning did take effect** — this is not a failed apply. Verified:
      user-scope `mcpServers` is down to 4 (`context7`, `filesystem`,
      `higgsfield`, `puppeteer`) plus `serena` from `.mcp.json`; `task-observer`
      is opt-in in global CLAUDE.md §17; 26 skills are parked in
      `~/Development/skills/`. The **~42k figure was the estimate that was
      wrong**, not the work. See the follow-up item below for where the tokens
      actually are.

- [x] **[P2]** **Closed 2026-07-31, 18:00 PST — the "~65k floor" this item was
      chasing does not exist, and its one real lever has now been pulled.**
      This item was written at 17:00 against the transcript number; the 17:15
      `/context` correction directly above supersedes it — the floor is
      **41.4k**, not ~65k, and the ~21k saving landed. Everything below was
      reasoning about a gap that measurement closed.

      **The one lever that was real — global `CLAUDE.md` — is done, and it is
      worth almost nothing.** The Serena/RTK doctrine duplication (the `[P3]`
      item in the section below) was trimmed to a pointer this session.
      Measured effect: `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` went **26,154 → 26,044 bytes**,
      110 bytes, roughly **30 tokens**. That is the honest number. The dedupe
      was worth doing because a rule stated twice drifts into two rules, not
      because it saves context — do not conflate the two again.

      **Do not spend further sessions pruning the floor.** 41.4k against a
      ~160k mean context per request means ~120k of a typical request is
      accumulated conversation. `/clear` discipline dominates every
      configuration lever available here by an order of magnitude. Plugin
      pruning, the "next lever" proposed below, would trade real capability
      for a low-thousands-of-tokens gain against that 120k.

      **Historical detail — the superseded model, kept for the trail.** The
      16:44 audit modelled MCP servers and `task-observer` as the dominant
      cost. Removing both moved the floor by roughly nothing, so the model was
      wrong. Observed in a fresh session (inventory counts, not token
      measurements — the system prompt still is not countable from inside a
