### Mid-session context investigation (2026-08-01, overnight, away mode)

Follow-on from the item directly above, after the owner retired the 42k
session-start target and redirected the effort to mid-session growth. Method
and rerun command: `~/.claude/scripts/measure-context-growth.py`
(`--project sensebridge --bash-shapes`), which attributes growth from **real
per-request `usage` deltas**, not character counts. Full write-up in
`tmp/AWAY-REPORT.md`.

**What the measurement found** (206 sessions, 20.4M tokens of measured growth):
a session grows a median **+75k** from its start floor (p90 +202k, max +402k),
and **78–83% of that growth is tool results**. By tool: **Bash 45.8%**
(9.36M tok over 6,945 batches, ~1,347 tok each), `Read` 15.9%, user prompts
7.9%, `Edit` 5.9%, model text 5.8%, `Write` 5.1%, `Agent` 1.6%. The floor is
~66k; growth is 1–5× the floor, so **growth is the right target** — the owner's
redirect was correct.

- [x] **[P3]** Decide whether RTK coverage is worth extending. Measured over
      6,645 project Bash calls: **9.9%** plain (RTK's own hook rewrites),
      **2.8%** already written `rtk`, **35.4%** RTK-coverable but shape-broken
      (closed by `.claude/hooks/prefer-rtk-shape.mjs` since 2026-07-31 — the
      ~855k historical cost predates it and is sunk), and **51.9% RTK cannot
      compact at all** (`cd` 1,167 calls/~418k, `echo` 414/~204k, `npx` 237,
      `for` 97, `python3` 126, `node` 136, `xcodebuild` 45, `curl` 46,
      `docker` 60). So RTK + the gap hook reach **at most ~48% of Bash calls by
      count**. Options: (a) accept it and treat RTK as a partial tool —
      recommended, since the uncovered set is mostly build/script runners whose
      output is already terse; (b) write compactors for `xcodebuild`/`npm`
      output, which is the only uncovered category with genuinely large output.
      Do **not** pick (b) without re-measuring first — it is ~15k tokens total.
      **Done 2026-08-01** — but the framing above was wrong, and re-measuring is
      what showed it. Two corrections. **(1) `&&` never broke RTK.** RTK's hook
      rewrites `cd /tmp && grep foo .` to `cd /tmp && rtk grep foo .`, and
      handles `;`, `||`, `&` the same way. **(2) The real breaker is a newline.**
      RTK rewrites only the *first line* of a multi-line command — `ls -la\ngrep
      foo src` came back as `rtk ls -la` with `grep` untouched — and rewrites
      nothing at all when line 1 is uncovered, which is exactly the `cd <abs
      path>` opening most of these calls. That is why `cd` looked like a 1,173-
      call / ~420k dead zone: **72% of the 1,810 `cd`-prefixed calls (1,298) are
      multi-line**, so they reached neither hook. Fixed by treating `\n` as a
      leaking shape in `.claude/hooks/prefer-rtk-shape.mjs`; four regression
      tests added, suite now 50 passing. Option (a) still stands for what is
      genuinely uncovered (`npx`, `xcodebuild`, `docker`) — do not build
      compactors without re-measuring.

- [x] **[P2]** `echo` scaffolding in compound Bash commands is the largest
      *self-inflicted* line item RTK cannot touch: **414 calls, ~204k tokens of
      raw output, ~507 tok each** — separators like `echo "=== SECTION ==="` and
      progress chatter that agents (including this one) emit inside `&&` chains.
      RTK has no `echo` compactor and never will; the fix is behavioral, not
      mechanical. Acceptance check: rerun
      `measure-context-growth.py --project sensebridge --bash-shapes` and
      confirm `echo` drops out of the top three of the "not an RTK command"
      table. Cheapest possible win — no code, just stop emitting them.
      **Done 2026-08-01** — owner chose "no separate action", and the reasoning
      held up: the `echo` spend is not really an `echo` problem. Those calls are
      almost all *multi-line*, and the newline fix in the item below now routes
      the whole block through `prefer-rtk-shape.mjs`, so every covered command
      around the `echo` gets compacted. Verified directly: `echo "=== a ==="\ngit
      status\nwc -l TODO.md` now rewrites to `rtk git status` + `rtk wc -l`. The
      `echo` line itself stays uncompacted and always will — at ~1% of total
      growth, a dedicated hook was not worth a fourth interceptor on the Bash
      path.

- [x] **[P2]** ~~**[Needs owner]**~~ `~/.claude/hooks/away-guard.sh` has two
      defects, both hit for real on 2026-08-01 while testing
      `prefer-rtk-shape.mjs`. **(1) It matches on substrings anywhere in the
      command text, including inside quoted arguments and test data.** A shell
      loop whose *literal string data* contained `git status` was blocked as a
      repository write, even though nothing would have executed it. This is the
      identical bug class that `prefer-rtk-shape.mjs` fixed on the same day with
      `maskHeredocs()`. **(2) It classifies read-only `git` subcommands as
      writes** — the block message said `git status` "writes to the repository
      or its history"; `status` is read-only, and the away-mode skill explicitly
      permits `status`/`diff`/`log`/`show`/`blame`/`rev-parse`/`ls-files`.
      Effect: false positives block legitimate read-only work while away, which
      trains agents to route around the guard — the opposite of what it is for.
      **Deliberately not fixed by an agent**: this is a user-global safety guard,
      and weakening one unattended is exactly what the away-mode contract
      forbids. Owner decision required on both. Acceptance check: a command
      containing `git status` inside quoted data passes, and a bare `git status`
      passes, while `git commit`/`git push` still block.
      **Fixed 2026-08-01**, on owner instruction, once away mode ended.
      **Defect (1) was real** and is fixed: quoted string literals are now
      blanked before classification (`strip_quoted()`), so `echo "run: git
      commit -m x"` passes while `git commit -m "msg"` still blocks. Whether it
      fired had been positional luck — the `git` in a double-quoted `echo`
      tokenized bare, while one in `grep -n 'git push' f` stayed glued to its
      quote and slipped through.
      **Defect (2) was NOT real — I reported it wrong.** Probing every form
      (`git status`, `--porcelain`, `git -C dir log`, `rtk git status`, `diff`)
      showed all are correctly allowed; `status` is in `GIT_READONLY` and always
      was. What actually happened was defect (1): my *test data* contained `git
      commit`, and I misattributed the resulting message.
      **A third defect surfaced while fixing these**, and it was fail-open:
      `sh -c "git commit -m x"` was **allowed**, because `subcommands_of()`
      tokenized `"git` — never equal to `git` — so no subcommand was ever
      extracted. Quote characters are now flattened alongside the shell
      operators, and commands that execute a string opt out of stripping so
      their contents stay visible. The guard is now strictly tighter than
      before, not looser.
      **A fourth: the disarm vocabulary missed plain English.** "End away mode
      as well" did not match `DISARM_RE`, so this very session kept injecting the
      armed banner after the owner had explicitly called it off. Added `end away
      mode`, `turn off away`, `exit away mode`, and `/away end|stop|done`.
      Regression suite added at `~/.claude/hooks/tests/away-guard.test.sh` —
      25 cases covering real writes, quoted-data mentions, read-only git,
      `sh -c`/`eval` smuggling, disarmed inertness, and the disarm vocabulary
      (including that an *arm* phrase must not disarm). It saves and restores
      real armed state on every exit path. It lives beside the guard in
      `~/.claude`, not in this repo, because gating this repo's CI on a
      user-global file would be wrong.
      **The identical bug class was found and fixed in a repo hook too**:
      `.claude/hooks/guard-main-commit.sh` denied any command whose text
      contained `git commit` — including prose and test fixtures — and its
      `sed 's/<<.*//'` heredoc cut only worked line-by-line, so heredoc bodies
      still leaked. Both fixed, with a 9-case suite at
      `.claude/hooks/tests/guard-main-commit.test.sh`.

- [x] **[P3]** `TODO.md` is the single most expensive file to read in the whole
      transcript corpus: **264k tokens across 182 reads** (~1,449 each), ahead of
      `docs/TOOLING.md` at 116k/64 — and TOOLING.md's cost is already addressed
      by the 2026-08-01 split. `cap-large-read.sh` keeps any single read to 500
      lines, so no individual read is catastrophic; the cost is **volume of
      reads**, not size per read. The `## Open queue (summary)` block at the top
      exists precisely so a reader does not need the whole file — reading
      `TODO.md` with `limit: 90` gets the summary and costs ~1.5k instead of
      ~33k. Consider stating that as a rule in `AGENTS.md` rather than adding
      more tooling. Related measurement: **62% of all `Read` calls pass no
      `offset`/`limit`**, and re-reading a file already read in the same session
      costs **215k tokens (13% of all Read spend)**.
      **Done 2026-08-01** — owner chose enforcement over an unenforced rule, on
      the grounds that `AGENTS.md` already says plenty that produced 182 reads
      anyway. `cap-large-read.sh` now recognises `TODO.md` and `COMPLETED.todo`
      as queue files: a Read passing **no** offset and **no** limit is clamped to
      120 lines (`CLAUDE_QUEUE_CHUNK_LINES`) and told to `Grep` for the `###`
      section heading instead. An explicit offset or limit means the caller knows
      where to look, so it falls through to the ordinary 500-line rule
      untouched. Verified across five cases: bare → 120 + guidance, `offset:800`
      → 500, `limit:40` → untouched, `COMPLETED.todo` bare → 120, a
      non-queue file → silent.

