### Safety-framing verdict on `fire_alarm`/`siren` alpha readiness (2026-08-06, 06:42 UTC)

- [x] **[P0]** **Custom sound classifier announces a fire alarm in a quiet
      room, at maximum confidence, at the app's strongest hedge tier.**
      `models/sound-classifier/combined-training-data/` has seven class
      directories and **no `background`/`other` class**, so the model is a
      closed-set softmax with no reject option. Measured on the **shipped**
      `app/SenseBridge/Resources/SenseBridgeSoundClassifier.mlmodel` through
      the production `SNAudioFileAnalyzer` path: low-level room hiss, white
      noise, and a 1 kHz tone each yield `fire_alarm` @ **1.000** on every
      window; a 200→4000 Hz sweep yields `siren` @ **1.000**. All clear
      `SoundClassificationRunner.defaultMinimumConfidence` (0.7) and map to
      `Certainty.high` via `Phrasing.swift:25-31`, so
      `SoundAlertsView.swift:72-82` speaks **"it looks like there's likely a
      fire alarm."** This is the `docs/SAFETY-FRAMING.md` Critical archetype —
      confidently-wrong, safety-adjacent physical-world output reaching the
      user.
      **Done 2026-08-06** — owner chose the full retrain over a same-day code
      mitigation. Added an eighth `background` class:
      `models/sound-classifier/gen-background-training-data.swift` synthesizes
