- [x] **[P2]** Verify the `Bash` deny globs actually match. Arg patterns are
      picomatch over the command string, so a compound line
      (`cd /tmp && git push --force`) may evade an anchored pattern — picomatch
      `*` does not cross `/`. A deny that matches nothing reports no error, and
      `kimi doctor` cannot detect it.
      **Done 2026-08-05** — verified, and the answer is **they do not match**.
      Kimi's engine was read out of the bundled JS rather than assumed:
      `Bash(...)` args route through `matchesGlobRuleSubject(ruleArgs,
      args.command)` → `globMatch(value, pattern)` → `picomatch.isMatch` with
      **no options**, against `picomatch@4.0.4`. Ran that exact algorithm
      offline against all 20 live `Bash(...)` patterns (extracted with a
      targeted `grep`; the API key never entered context), cross-checked on
      picomatch 4.0.5 and 2.3.2 — identical results. **All 12 testable rules
      MISS the `cd /tmp && <cmd>` form**, including `Bash(git *)` and
      `Bash(gh *)`, so the owner-gating on `git`/`gh` is trivially bypassable.
      Worse, `Bash(*rm -rf /*)` and `Bash(*rm -fr /*)` miss even the plain
      forms (`rm -rf /`, `rm -rf /tmp/x`, `rm -rf ~/foo`) because the literal
      `/` forces a segment-count match, and `Bash(*curl*|*sh*)` misses any
      pipeline containing a URL. Verified replacement form
      `{*,**/*}NEEDLE{*,*/**}` matches every case; full table, negative
      controls, and the per-rule rewrite are in
      `audits/security/20260806-064337-kimi-code-harness-hardening.md`
      (findings #2/#3). **Rewrite applied 2026-08-06** — owner authorized it
      directly. All 20 live `Bash(...)` patterns in `~/.kimi-code/config.toml`
      converted to the `{*,**/*}NEEDLE{*,*/**}` form (the 2 pipe-to-shell
      patterns, `curl`/`wget`, got the analogous `NEEDLE1{*,*/**}|{*,**/*}NEEDLE2`
      treatment — not covered by the audit's validated table, so re-verified
      independently against picomatch before writing, all 20 patterns pass
      their positive and negative controls). Verified: `kimi doctor` still
      reports the config valid, file permissions unaffected by the rewrite
      (`600`).

