## Archived 2026-08-13

### TODO burn-down pass — findings (2026-08-12, 22:00 PST)

- [x] **[P3]** **`~/.claude/hooks/guard-protected-paths.sh` denies on a
      protected *word* anywhere in the command string, not on the delete's
      actual target.** Hit twice this session. First: a compound command whose
      delete target was `app/project.yml`, but which later passed `audits` and
      `sessions` to `grep` as directory-exclusion flags — `PROTECTED_SEGMENTS`
      matched those flags and denied the whole call. Re-running the bare delete
      as its own command passed, which confirms the target was never the
      problem. Second, and more clearly: writing *this very item* was blocked,
      because the paragraph you are reading quotes that command inside a
      heredoc — no delete was being performed at all, only described. That is
      the same class as the `away-guard.sh` heredoc false positive already
      logged in the 2026-08-11 section below, now with a second guard showing
      it. Fail-closed-on-a-word is the right default for a destructive guard,
      so this is deliberately **not** a "loosen the regex" request. The fix
      worth making is to scan only the delete verb's own arguments, and to name
      the matched token in the denial so an operator can tell a real block from
      this one. Workaround meanwhile: write prose containing these words with
      an editor tool rather than a shell heredoc.
      **Half done 2026-08-12 — the first case is fixed, the second is
      deliberately not.** `names_protected()` now skips tokens beginning with
      `-`: an option value is a filter, not a target, so excluding a protected
      directory from a search no longer denies the command's real operand. Four
      ALLOW and six DENY regression cases added to
      `~/.claude/hooks/tests/guard-protected-paths.test.sh`; the whole suite
      passes (`guard-protected-delete: 32 passed`), including every case that
      must still deny — a `cd` into a protected tree followed by an
      innocuous-looking delete, `rsync --delete`, `git clean -fdx`, and
      `find … -delete`.
      **The originally-proposed fix — "scan only the delete verb's own
      arguments" — turns out to be wrong and was not implemented.** It would
      allow `cd ~/Vault && rm -rf .obsidian`, where the protected path is not
      the delete's operand at all; the whole-command scan exists precisely for
      that shape, and the test file has a case for it. Skipping option tokens
      is the part that is provably safe.
      **The heredoc case stays open and should probably stay unfixed.** Prose
      quoting a destructive command inside a heredoc is still denied, because
      heredoc bodies are still scanned — and that is what catches a real
      `bash << EOF … EOF` wrapping a delete (also a test case). Exempting
      heredoc bodies would open a genuine hole to remove an annoyance. The
      **The other half is now done too:** the denial names the matched token
      (`Matched on: audits`), via a `first_protected_token()` second scan run
      only on the deny path — so the hot path stays a plain boolean and
      `names_protected`'s subshell does not have to smuggle a value out. A
      denial is already fatal; one extra pass costs nothing. With the token
      named, a heredoc false positive is self-evident from the message instead
      of needing the command bisected by hand, which is most of what made the
      remaining case expensive.

- [x] **[P1]** **Four `website/` checks cannot run on this machine:
      Puppeteer's Chrome will not unpack.** `check:csp`, `check:consent`,
      `check:scene-drag`, and `check:bfcache` all fail identically with
      `Could not find Chrome (ver. 148.0.7778.97)`. The cache holds only
      `mac_arm-151.0.7922.77` — a newer build than this Puppeteer resolves —
      so the four have been silently unrunnable, and the earlier `check:audio`
      failure meant `npm run check` never reached them to say so.
      **New evidence, and it contradicts the existing note:** installing 148
      under **Node 22** (`~/.nvm/versions/node/v22.22.3`) produced the same
      truncated result as Node 26 — 41 files and 448K instead of ~370M, with
      the `.framework` never unpacked, so the binary dies in `dlopen`. The
      "Node 26 truncates the browser install" diagnosis is therefore
      **incomplete**: the truncation is not Node-version-specific and the real
      cause is still unknown (candidates: the extractor, a partial CDN
      response, or a sandbox/quarantine interaction). The partial 148 directory
      was deleted again so the machine is exactly as it was found — the failure
      mode is "absent", not "corrupt". **Next step:** try
      `PUPPETEER_SKIP_DOWNLOAD` plus a manual unzip of the Chrome-for-Testing
      archive, or pin `puppeteer` to a version whose expected build matches the
      151 already cached. **Verify a fix:** all four scripts pass, and
      `npm run check` in `website/` reaches the end.
      **Done 2026-08-12** — root-caused and fixed. **It is the extractor, not
      the download and not the Node version.** Fetching the same archive
      directly returns HTTP 200 and a complete 169MB zip that passes
      `unzip -t` with 651 entries; Puppeteer's own installer then writes 41 of
      them and exits 0 with its usual success line, leaving
      `Google Chrome for Testing Framework.framework` absent so the binary dies
      in `dlopen`. Unpacking the identical archive with macOS `ditto` produces a
      working 357MB install. New `website/scripts/install-chrome.mjs`
      (`npm --prefix website run chrome:install`) does that reliably: it reads
      the expected version from `puppeteer-core`'s own `PUPPETEER_REVISIONS` so
      it can never drift from what Puppeteer launches, replaces a truncated
      install, refuses anything under 250MB, and verifies by **running the
      binary** rather than trusting an exit code — a size check alone would not
      have caught how this actually presented. **Verified:** all four scripts
      now pass, and so do `test:a11y` and `doctor`, which were blocked by the
      same cause. The full download path was exercised end-to-end, not just the
      already-installed path. Only `check:audio` still fails in `website/`, for
      the unrelated reason above.

