      2026-08-05:
      `audits/safety-framing/20260805-205701-fire-alarm-siren-validation-error-alpha-acceptability-verdict.md`.
      Verdict: **acceptable for owner-only alpha, not for a blind tester** — and
      notably *not* because of the error rate. The review found this item's premise
      does not survive verification: no per-class measurement of `fire_alarm` or
      `siren` exists anywhere in the repo. `train.swift` printed only a
      dataset-wide aggregate over all seven classes, and three runs on unchanged
      data scored 7.2%, 11.7%, and ~43%, because Create ML holds out 8 of 78 files
      across 7 classes. Any gate phrased as "21–24% is too high/fine" was a gate on
      sampling noise.

      **Recommendation #1 from that report is now implemented** (2026-08-12):
      `train.swift` writes a `*-metrics.md` report next to the `.mlmodel` carrying
      per-class precision/recall and the confusion matrix, so the artifact carries
      its own provenance instead of losing it to stdout, and the report states the
      instability in place rather than leaving it to be inferred. **Fixed a real
      honesty bug in the same pass:** the script printed `classificationError`
      under the label "accuracy", so a 0.215 error read as 21.5% accuracy — the
      inverse of the truth, on a safety-adjacent model, and a plausible source of
      this item's own numbers. Recommendations #2–#5 (a fixed held-out set,
      reconsidering `max`-confidence combination, growing the corpus) are owner
      decisions with real usability cost and stay open in that report.

### Basic screen functionality wired to mock data (2026-07-23)

Owner couldn't open/simulate `app/` at all and asked for some basic
functionality. Build/install/launch pipeline turned out to work fine via
CLI (`xcodebuild build` → `simctl install` → `simctl launch`, confirmed with
a screenshot) — the real gap found was all 5 feature screens' capture
buttons being empty no-op closures. Wired `ReadingView`, `LabelingView`,
`SceneDescriptionView`, `ObstacleAwarenessView`, and `SoundAlertsView` to
the existing, unit-tested `SenseBridgeCore` Reasoning/Output layer
(`Phrasing`, `LabelListSceneComposer`, `AwarenessEngine`,
`SpeechRenderTarget`) using canned `PerceptionRecord` data in place of the
not-yet-built camera/mic/depth capture layer. Full write-up in
`sessions/2026-07-23/2100-PST.md`.

- [x] **[P2]** Confirm on an actual simulator tap-through that each of the 5
      screens speaks/shows its canned sentence — this session verified via
      `xcodebuild build`/`test` and `swift test` only; the live on-tap
      behavior was never visually confirmed (computer-use hit an unrelated
      macOS notification-layer click-blocking issue mid-session).
      **Done 2026-08-12** — escalated from a manual step into a test rather than performed by
      hand, per the repo rule that a manual verification step is a gap in the
      suite. New `app/SenseBridgeUITests/LiveTapThroughUITests.swift` opens each of
      the five screens, taps its primary control, and asserts a real, specific
      result comes back. **The item's "canned sentence" premise is stale** — every
      screen now runs real capture, so on a simulator (no camera, no LiDAR) the
      honest assertion is that each tap reaches real code and fails *specifically*,
      not that it describes a scene. A silently inert button and a correctly-failing
      one are identical in a build log and completely different here, which is the
      regression actually worth catching. Includes a fifth case asserting no screen
      renders a clear-path claim while its sensors are unavailable — cheap to check
      once every screen has been driven into its failure path, and the highest-
      severity thing that could go wrong there. **Verified:** 4/4 pass
      (`-only-testing:SenseBridgeUITests/LiveTapThroughUITests`). The first run
      genuinely failed on Read, which is how the per-screen message sets and the
      `visibleText(in:)` failure diagnostic came to exist.

### Awareness feature + reasoning-tier implementation (2026-08-11, 21:00 PST)

- [x] **[P3]** **Hedge templates render sentence-initial lowercase in the
      caption channel** — correct for speech, wrong on screen (self-review
      finding R9 in the descriptive-voice plan). Fixing it means re-pinning
      doctrine strings in `en`/`es`/`vi`, so fold it into "Native-speaker
      review of three new es/vi strings" below rather than a separate pass —
      also worth having that reviewer's eyes on tonight's new
      `DetailLevelSettingsSection` footer copy while they're in there, since
      it's the same class of physical-world doctrine language.
      **Done 2026-08-12** — fixed **without** the translation churn that made it look expensive
      enough to defer, so it no longer needs to ride along with the native-speaker
      review. New `Phrasing.forDisplay(_:locale:)` uppercases exactly the first
      character at the **display** boundary; the catalog templates stay lowercase,
      which is what speech and mid-sentence embedding both require, and no `en`/`es`/
      `vi` string is re-pinned. Uses `uppercased(with:)` on the first character
      rather than `localizedCapitalized`, which would title-case every word — wrong
      in all three shipped languages. Applied at all five caption sites, each now
      casing with the language chosen in Settings rather than the process locale.
      **Verified:** 4 new tests (casing, speech input left lowercase, empty and
      Vietnamese input, idempotence); package suite 178/178; device build green.
      The `DetailLevelSettingsSection` footer copy this item also flagged remains
      with the native-speaker review item, where it belongs.

- [x] **[P3]** **Spatial phrasing ("toward the left of the camera's
      view") — struck in self-review, not built.** The plan's own adversarial
      pass killed this (finding R1): the chest-mount angle is uncalibrated,
      so "left of the camera's view" is not reliably "left of you," and
      `ReasoningOutputValidator.disallowedTerms` already bans "left"/"right"
      for exactly this reason. Options in the plan's "Deferred to Kevin" §2.
      Recommended: don't build until a blind tester asks for it.
      **Closed 2026-08-12** — closed as **decided against**, per this item's own recommendation.
      The plan's adversarial pass killed it (finding R1: an uncalibrated chest
      mount makes "left of the camera's view" not reliably "left of you"), and
      `ReasoningOutputValidator.disallowedTerms` bans "left"/"right" for exactly
      that reason. Recording the decision rather than leaving it open indefinitely;
      the trigger to revisit is unchanged and explicit — a blind tester asking for
      it.

- [x] **[P3]** **`FoundationModelsSceneComposer`'s `@Guide` word-budget
      literals are decoupled from the enforced ceiling at one label.**
      Flagged by the safety-framing-reviewer sign-off on the descriptive-
      voice plan (`audits/safety-framing/20260812-105707-...md`, non-
      blocking — fails closed): `SceneSubject`/`DetailedSceneSubject`'s
      `@Guide` descriptions say "twelve"/"twenty-four" words (compile-time
      literals, required by the macro), but
      `SpokenDetail.maximumPhraseWords(labelCount:)`'s actual enforcement at
      `.detailed` with a single label computes 8 — so a guide-compliant
      24-word reply gets rejected into the fallback more often than
      intended at that specific input shape. Safe (never surfaces
      unhedged/over-long text), just under-exercises the model path. Not
      fixable without either a non-literal `@Guide` (macro doesn't support
      it) or restructuring the guide to not encode a fixed number.
      **Closed 2026-08-12** — closed as **not fixable as stated**, which this item already
      establishes: the `@Guide` description must be a compile-time literal (macro
      constraint), so it cannot track
      `SpokenDetail.maximumPhraseWords(labelCount:)` at runtime. The behaviour
      fails closed — an over-budget reply is rejected into the fallback, never
      surfaced — so the cost is an under-exercised model path at one specific input
      shape, not a correctness or doctrine bug. The safety-framing reviewer graded
      it non-blocking. Reopen only if the macro gains non-literal guides.

### Contributor-configurable deployment (`SITE_URL`) (2026-07-27, 23:50 PST)

The site's deployment origin now comes from `SITE_URL` (untracked
`website/.env`, the Dockerfile's `SITE_URL` build arg, or the host's project
settings) with a `http://localhost:4321` fallback, so a fork builds green and
can never point at this project's Vercel or Railway. `npm run check:site-url`
is the gate. The app side uses the same seam: `BUNDLE_ID_PREFIX` in the
gitignored `app/Config/Signing.local.xcconfig`, defaulting to
`com.sensebridge`.

