### Signal Spine motion batch — accessibility review follow-ups (2026-07-18)

- [x] **[P3]** Audio-narration ↔ spine-pulse sync (design roadmap #3):
      drive the Signal Spine pulse from narration progress while the
      natural-voice player runs. **Unblocked 2026-07-25** — `/audio/main.mp3`
      now exists and the natural-voice control renders on `/`.
      **Done 2026-08-12** — built. While the natural-voice narration plays, the Signal Spine
      follows the *narration* instead of the scroll, then hands control back when
      it stops. The spine's job is to show where you are in the story; when the
      page is reading itself aloud, the audio is where you are, and a spine still
      tracking the scrollbar points at the wrong thing.

      Wired through a `sensebridge:narration` `CustomEvent` on `document` rather
      than a direct call between the two scripts, so either side can be absent and
      the other still works — `read-aloud.ts` is progressive enhancement that must
      run with the motion layer disabled (reduced motion, a JS error, a page with
      no spine), and the motion layer must not have to know an audio element
      exists. The scroll driver is `disable(false)`d rather than killed, so the
      pulse resumes from where the narration left it instead of snapping to the top.
      **Verified:** typecheck, lint, format, build, and the browser-driven
      `check:csp`/`check:consent`/`check:scene-drag`/`check:bfcache`/`test:a11y`
      all pass — a thrown error in `motion.ts` fails those. **Not verified:** that
      the pulse visibly tracks the audio, which needs a look in a browser with the
      narration playing.

- [x] **[P3]** Read-aloud per-section segmentation + polite stage
      announcements ("Now: what it does today" etc., derived from h2 text) in
      the existing `#read-aloud-status` live region. Skipped in the motion
      batch because `read-aloud.ts` speaks one monolithic utterance —
      requires restructuring it into per-section segments first.
      **Done 2026-08-12** — `read-aloud.ts` now speaks one utterance per top-level `<section>`
      instead of one for the whole page, announcing each section's own `h2` as
      "Now: the section heading" in the existing polite live region as it starts. Segments are
      built by walking `main.children` rather than `querySelectorAll("section")`, so
      document order is preserved, loose content before the first section is kept as
      an unheaded preamble, and a nested section can never be read twice; a page
      with no sections falls back to a single whole-page segment, so this can never
      read *less* than the old behaviour. Chained through `onend` with an
      `activeStop` guard, because `cancel()` also fires `onend` on some engines and
      the chain would otherwise march on after the user pressed stop. One new
      `en`/`es`/`vi` message (`read_aloud_now_section`). This also unblocks the
      spine-pulse sync item above, which was waiting on exactly this restructuring.

### Deferred from docs/planning/ (2026-08-07, 14:36 PST)

- [x] **[P2]** **Thermal-state backoff for sustained camera/depth/inference
      use.** Source: `SENSEBRIDGE-04-ENGINEERING-QUALITY.md` §17
      "Observability & Reliability" → "Performance monitoring": "Watch
      thermal state (`ProcessInfo.thermalState`) and back off sustained
